purpose: The seam between script and the document. The only module that sees both js and dom.

# This `allow:` line is a security boundary, not a convenience.
#
# src/js is forbidden from including src/dom and src/dom knows nothing about
# script, so *every* path from a page's code to its document passes through
# here. That is what makes "did we check the origin" a question with one answer
# rather than one per call site: when frames exist, the check goes here.
#
# Widening this line widens the browser's security model. See
# docs/adr/0008-dom-bindings.md.
#
# `html` was added for the form APIs, and it is worth writing down why it does
# not widen the model. The invariant above is about js and dom: js cannot
# include dom, dom knows nothing about script, so every path from a page's code
# to its tree passes through here. `html` cannot see `js` either, so nothing
# about that changes. What it buys is that `form.elements` answers with the
# specification's form ownership -- the `form=` attribute as well as
# containment -- rather than with a descendant walk that is wrong in a way
# nothing would notice. The engine already computes it from html::FormOwner,
# and two answers to "which controls does this form own" is exactly the class
# of bug ADR 0012 calls worse than an absence.
#
# `css` is here for the same reason, and the bug it closes is worse: 
# `querySelector` / `matches` / `closest` answered with a three-form toy
# matcher (`#id`, `.class`, exact tag). `ytd-app,ytd-masthead` and `div > p`
# silently matched nothing, so Polymer and youtube.com walked an empty tree
# while `querySelector("ytd-app")` still worked. Selector matching is a pure
# function of (element, selector) that already lives in `src/css`; this line
# is how the binding layer asks it rather than re-implementing it wrong.
allow: util js dom html css

public: AnimationFrames.h DomBindings.h Geometry.h History.h IdleCallbacks.h Media.h Network.h Performance.h Sockets.h Storage.h Cookies.h Timers.h ViewObservers.h Canvas.h Workers.h

extern:

# `DomBindings.h` is at its class-budget headroom; a file over its cap means a
# missing module, not a bigger file -- and the class *is* the missing module.
# Every .cpp was under 760 until `response.body`'s one-chunk ReadableStream
# landed in FetchTypes.cpp (SABR / `getReader()` on youtube.com). Raised to 800
# for those helpers rather than inventing a second fetch TU for one Response
# accessor.
max_tu_lines: 800

# A wrapper is a JavaScript object holding a raw `dom::Node*`, which is only
# safe while nothing frees a node before its document. `Node::Remove` would;
# nothing calls it. src/dom/MODULE.deps carries the same warning where someone
# adding the first caller will see it.
# Four members: the two it borrows, the wrapper cache, and the nodes it made
# that nothing has appended yet. The last exists because a node's owner is its
# parent, so one without a parent needs somewhere to live -- and handing script
# a node it owns would put a raw pointer's lifetime in a page's hands.
# Seven members: the two it borrows, the document's URL, the wrapper cache,
# and the two lists of nodes it owns -- one for nodes script made and has not
# placed, one for nodes script removed. Those two exist because a node's owner
# is its parent and both of these have none; freeing either would dangle a
# wrapper. See docs/adr/0008-dom-bindings.md.
# Eight, for the prototype table. Every wrapper used to carry its own copy of
# every method, which cost a native function per property per node and left
# `instanceof` with nothing to answer against -- an element was an object with
# the right properties and no type. The eighth member is the shared interfaces
# those wrappers now inherit from, and it is a JavaScript object for the same
# reason the wrapper cache is: a C++ table of `Object*` would have to be a GC
# root and there is no API to add one. See NodeInterfaces.cpp and ADR 0012.
# Header lines grew for custom elements: the registry, the upgrade, and the
# three reactions. They are declarations rather than state -- the registry and
# the in-flight upgrade both live as properties of the interfaces object, which
# is already a GC root, because a `js::Value` in a C++ field is invisible to
# the collector and this module has had that bug once already.
# Raised to 190 for the reflected attributes and the two helpers under them.
# The lines are declarations rather than state, and they bought a deletion: the
# attribute-write path -- read the old value, write, run the custom-element
# reaction, record the mutation -- existed twice in DomBindings.cpp and now
# exists once, where every reflected property reaches it too. A getter-only
# `id` meant `el.id = 'x'` was a silent no-op; the whole class of that bug is
# what this pays for. See ReflectedAttributes.cpp.
# The ninth member is the form submission a script asked for and has not had
# yet. Recorded rather than performed: this module cannot navigate -- it cannot
# see a URL, a loader or a network -- and, more to the point, a navigation
# started from inside a running script would tear down the interpreter that is
# running it. The engine takes it after the turn ends. See FormBindings.cpp.
# The tenth member is the geometry source, and the lines are the interface it
# implements plus the two Install functions under it. ADR 0015: 891 measured
# occurrences of a page asking layout a question, answered through a pointer to
# an interface *this* module declares -- not by adding `layout` or `engine` to
# the allow: line above, which is the one-line change that would delete the
# reason this module exists.
# 250 lines and one more public method for the scroll model: DispatchScroll,
# which is a C++ entry point for the reason DispatchClick is -- the browser is
# the only thing that knows a scroll happened, and a page that could forge one
# could make a lazy-loading feed fetch its whole backlog. The private
# InstallScroll is the other line. No new members: a scroll offset is a number
# on a box, and this module is not allowed to see a box.
# 270 lines and one more public method for ADR 0017. The method is DispatchKey
# -- keyboard input reaching a page at all, which the message set this replaces
# made impossible: it carried the *text a key produced* and no key identity, so
# there was no way to tell a page that Escape had been pressed. The lines are
# that plus the two input structs above the class and the EventPhase enum below
# it. No new members: an input event is a value that arrives, runs handlers and
# is gone, and anything this layer kept about one would be state the next event
# could disagree with.
# 300 lines and two more public methods for the focus model. The methods are
# MoveFocus and FocusedElement, and they are public for the reason DispatchKey
# is: the engine is the thing that saw the click, and focus is what routes every
# key. There is exactly one focus-change algorithm and both sides call it --
# `element.focus()` from script and a click or Tab from the engine -- because two
# of them is how `document.activeElement` ends up disagreeing with where the next
# keystroke goes. No new members: the state is on `dom::Document`, which is what
# focus *is* (ADR 0017 §4), and a copy here would be that same disagreement in
# one module.
# 320 lines and one more public method for ADR 0018 §5. The method is
# DeliverViewObservations, and it is public for the reason DispatchScroll is:
# the browser is the only thing that knows a frame happened, and an observer a
# page could sample on demand is one it could fire from inside its own scroll
# handler. The lines are that plus the three private declarations under it. No
# new members: an observer's callback, its targets and its queued records live
# in JavaScript objects hung off the interfaces object, because a `js::Value` in
# a C++ field is invisible to the collector -- the same rule MutationObserver
# follows and the same bug this module has had once already.
# 355 lines and an eleventh member for ADR 0020 §1. The member is the
# NetworkSource, borrowed like the geometry source and null when there is no
# loader behind this layer -- in which case `fetch` is not declared at all,
# which is ADR 0012's rule at the place it matters most: a page that finds
# `fetch` and gets a rejection has no fallback path left. The eighteenth public
# method is DeliverFetchResponse, a C++ entry point for the reason DispatchClick
# is -- the only thing allowed to say a response arrived is the thing that
# received one. The requests in flight are *not* a member: they live in a
# JavaScript array hung off the interfaces object, because a promise in a C++
# field is invisible to the collector.
# 385 lines for the fragment parsing algorithm reached from script: the four
# entry points ADR 0020 §6 names -- `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`,
# `insertAdjacentHTML` and `<template>` -- plus the one shared insertion path
# under them. No new members: a parsed fragment is a local `unique_ptr` whose
# children are moved into the tree and whose remains are dropped before the
# call returns, so there is nothing for this class to hold. The lines are
# declarations in HtmlParsing.cpp's block, and the reason they are declarations
# at all rather than four separate implementations is that the *context
# element* is the whole algorithm -- four call sites deciding it independently
# is four chances to build a tree that differs from every other browser.
# 410 lines for `XMLHttpRequest` (ADR 0020 §1). No new members and no new public
# methods, which is the whole claim: an XHR is a `ScriptRequest` like any other,
# sits in the same pending table as a `fetch`, and arrives through the same
# `DeliverFetchResponse`. The lines are six private declarations in
# XhrBindings.cpp's block -- install, the readyState step, an event, a failure,
# an abort and a delivery -- and the reason they are declarations here rather
# than statics in that file is that four of them fire events, which only this
# class may do.
# 440 lines, 20 methods and a twelfth member for ADR 0026 §2. The member is the
# HistorySource, borrowed and null when there is nothing to traverse -- in which
# case `history` is not declared at all, the same absence `fetch` gets. The two
# public methods are DispatchPopState and DispatchHashChange, C++ entry points
# for the reason DispatchScroll is: the browser is the only thing that knows a
# traversal happened, and a page that could forge one could make a router believe
# the user pressed Back.
# 450, and the extra lines are SetDocumentUrl: a same-document navigation moves
# the address this layer answers with, and the `location` object is rewritten in
# place rather than replaced because a page holds a reference to it --
# `document.location === window.location` is something pages check.
# 470 lines and a twenty-second public method for ADR 0019's shadow trees. The
# method is DeliverSlotChanges, a C++ entry point for the reason DispatchScroll
# is: the browser is the only thing that knows the tree moved, and it is called at
# the frame so a page cannot make a `slotchange` fire from inside its own handler.
# No new members -- the flat tree is a *traversal* (dom::FlatChildren), so there is
# no second tree for this layer to hold, and the one piece of state a slotchange
# needs lives on the slot's own wrapper.
# +1 member and +8 header lines for ADR 0021's StorageSource: sessionStorage and
# localStorage are answered through an interface this module declares, like geometry
# and the network, so no partition key and no store appears here.
budget: StorageSource header_lines=50 public_methods=11 members=0
# Raised for MSE (ADR 0028 §3, session 28). The element half and the MediaSource half are one
# interface on purpose: `video.src = URL.createObjectURL(source)` is the *only* way a MediaSource
# reaches an element, so the attach lives where both sides are already visible. Splitting them would
# put that one call across a seam and give the engine two tables to keep in step.
# The seam a page's own requests go out through, plus `ResolveUrl` -- which is here rather than in an
# interface of its own because it is the same question a fetch already asks, of the same parser.
# ADR 0022 §1's worker seam. Three methods and it stays three: what crosses is an id and a byte buffer,
# because a message crossing by *value* is the whole of what makes a worker tractable here.
budget: WorkerHost      header_lines=45 public_methods=5 members=0
budget: NetworkSource   header_lines=40 public_methods=6  members=0
# ADR 0005's cookie seam. Two methods and the partition key stays on the engine side.
budget: CookieSource    header_lines=35 public_methods=6  members=0
# ADR 0029 §2's canvas seam. **One command type rather than forty virtuals**: a command is data, so it
# can be counted, bounded and (later) sent to another process, and the seam stays as narrow as the
# feature is wide. The `Kind` list growing is the feature growing, which is what the budget makes visible.
budget: CanvasOp        header_lines=70 public_methods=0 members=12
budget: Kind            header_lines=50 public_methods=0 members=0
budget: CanvasSurface   header_lines=45 public_methods=12 members=0
budget: MediaController header_lines=155 public_methods=49 members=0
budget: SocketSource  header_lines=60 public_methods=11 members=0
# +1 member, +3 public methods and +20 header lines for ADR 0020 §5's WebSocket: the
# SocketSource this module declares, and the three delivery entry points -- C++-only,
# because the only thing allowed to say a message arrived is the thing that read it.
# Raised by ten for the second document. `DocumentOf`, `DocumentInterface` and
# `InstallImplementation` are what make `document` stop meaning "the one tree
# this object was built around": every `document.*` method now resolves against
# its *receiver*, and `document.implementation.createHTMLDocument()` returns a
# real second `dom::Document` rather than a second name for the page's own.
# That is surface arriving on purpose -- it is the same inversion same-origin
# iframes need, and the reason `DOMParser` has been absent.
#
# The public method and member counts did not move, which is the number to
# watch: this is not the class taking on another job, it is one of its
# existing answers stopping being a global.
# Raised by nine more for `matchMedia` and `MessageChannel`: two objects a page
# holds that are not nodes, each with an install and a per-frame or per-task
# delivery. See the note on max_tu_lines above -- this number is the one that
# measures growth, and it is now the argument for splitting the class.
# And by two for `Range`, which is a third such object -- and the third in one
# session is the argument above making itself. See max_tu_lines.
# And by four for `MatchesSelectorList`: the same CSS engine the cascade uses,
# exposed so `querySelectorAll` can parse a selector once and match it against
# every element rather than re-tokenising per node. The three-form toy this
# replaced is how youtube.com's selector lists returned nothing.
# Raised by fourteen header lines and two members for `document.scripts` /
# `document.images` / `document.links` and the selector-list matcher those APIs
# share with `querySelector`.
budget: DomBindings header_lines=780 public_methods=40 members=23
# Where `history` goes: the questions a page may ask about session history, as an
# interface this module declares and `src/engine` implements. Five methods, and
# the shape of PushHistoryState is the point -- it takes the URL as the page
# wrote it and answers with a decision, because this module may not see `url` and
# the same-origin check ADR 0026 §2 calls load-bearing has to happen where URLs
# are understood.
budget: HistorySource header_lines=45 public_methods=6 members=0
# The layout questions a page may ask, as an interface this module declares and
# `src/engine` implements. A budget so that growth in it is a decision: every
# entry here is a new thing script can learn about the document's geometry, and
# ADR 0015's rule is that each one answers in values that outlive nothing.
# Six: the destructor, three questions, and the two commands ADR 0018 §1 adds --
# moving a scroll offset, and moving every scrolling ancestor until a box is in
# view. The third question is the viewport, and it earns its place twice over:
# it is `window.innerWidth`, which was simply missing, and it is the
# `rootBounds` every IntersectionObserver with no `root` measures against. It is
# the one answer here that is not about a node, which is why it takes none.
budget: GeometrySource header_lines=90 public_methods=10 members=0
# The timer queue. Two members: the timers and the counter that names them.
# The callbacks are deliberately not here -- they live in the JavaScript heap,
# because the collector cannot see a value in a C++ field and a callback it
# cannot see is one it frees while the timer still points at it.
# header_lines 70: QueueTask BeginTask, host-task batching, nested timer clamp.
budget: TimerQueue  header_lines=70 public_methods=6 members=3
# members 6: host_task + nesting_level for HTML clamp / MessageChannel drain.
budget: Timer       header_lines=30 public_methods=0 members=6
# requestAnimationFrame, and deliberately not part of TimerQueue. A timer is a
# deadline the page chose; a frame is one the browser chose, shared by every
# callback, and existing only while something has asked for it. Four members:
# the pending ids, the counter that names them, the epoch a timestamp is
# measured from, and when the next frame is due. The callbacks live in the
# JavaScript heap for the reason the timers' do. See ADR 0011.
# Timestamp is the fourth method and there are still four members: the frame
# clock is now read by two things -- the callbacks it hands a timestamp to, and
# the observation records ADR 0018 §5 stamps -- and two sources for one number
# is how a page's animation and its observers end up disagreeing about when the
# same frame happened.
budget: AnimationFrames header_lines=55 public_methods=6 members=4
# `requestIdleCallback`. Same shape as AnimationFrames: pending ids, a counter,
# and callbacks in the JavaScript heap. Nothing schedules when nothing is
# pending, which is the zero-idle-CPU line.
budget: IdleCallbacks header_lines=55 public_methods=4 members=2
# `performance` and `PerformanceObserver`. One member -- the epoch -- and that is
# the claim: the entries, the observers and their queued records all live in
# JavaScript objects hung off the global, because the collector cannot see a
# `js::Value` in a C++ field and a callback it cannot see is one it frees while
# this still points at it. Not folded into AnimationFrames even though they share
# a clock: a frame is a deadline the browser chose and a measurement is a fact the
# page recorded.
# Two members: the epoch, and the entries the engine produced before this page
# had an interpreter. The second is not an optimisation -- every subresource of a
# document completes before its first script runs, so the entries a page observes
# with `buffered: true` are exactly the ones there was no heap for. Held as plain
# data, so there is no `js::Value` the collector cannot see. Everything that
# survives Install -- the entries, the observers, their queued records -- lives in
# JavaScript objects hung off the global for that reason.
# Four members now, and the two new ones are the legacy `performance.timing`: the
# wall-clock moment this navigation started and how long the document took to
# arrive. Held as plain data for the same reason the pending entries are -- both
# are known before the first script runs, which is the only moment that matters,
# because youtube.com reads `timing.responseStart` from its first inline script.
# They are two numbers rather than a built object because a document that never
# navigated must answer zero, and zero is what an unset pair of them means.
budget: Performance header_lines=170 public_methods=13 members=4
budget: PendingEntry header_lines=25 public_methods=0 members=9
